\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Three-way comparison of emission distributions and duration specifications for SPY.} Standard errors in parentheses; bold marks the best value per row. The HSMM is a hidden semi-Markov-style model adapted from \citet  {bullaBulla2006} ($K = 8$ states, negative-binomial dwell times with a geometric fallback, and Student-$t$ emissions); HMM-WJ: hidden Markov model with jumps ($N = 100$). All entries are computed from $1{,}000$ simulated paths at significance level $\alpha = 0.05$. Kolmogorov--Smirnov (KS), Anderson--Darling (AD), and mean absolute error in the autocorrelation function (ACF-MAE) are defined in Online Appendix~\ref {sec:supp-validation}. Autocorrelation error compares the observed curve with the ensemble-average curve, using lags $1$--$252$ in training and $1$--$248$ in the holdout.}}{22}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:emission_hsmm}{{S4}{22}{}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Hybrid composition method broken out by branch.} $N_{\mathrm  {tickers}}$: number of tickers assigned to each branch by the $R^2_{\mathrm  {preserve}} = 0.80$ threshold. The clipped variance-preserving branch does not trigger anywhere in the universe ($\rho _i < 0.90$ for all tickers) and is omitted. The two trackers in the $R^2$-preserving branch (QQQ and SPYG) had median absolute errors of $0.005$ for $\beta $ and $0.001$ for $R^2$, with a KS pass rate of $100\%$.}}{23}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:branch}{{S5}{23}{}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Restricted Gaussian check of the $R^2$-preserving branch.} Both series used the observed SPY path and independent Gaussian residual draws; the composer used unit residual scale and centered its draw. No full-return model was fitted. Entries are medians over $100$ replications per cell with cross-replication standard deviations in parentheses. Loading and $R^2$ were recovered by ordinary least squares. The nominal Kolmogorov--Smirnov (KS) pass rate is the percentage of comparisons with $p>0.05$; its reference calibration was not established for this design. Every cell used the $R^2$-preserving branch.}}{23}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:tracker_grid}{{S6}{23}{}{table.6}{}}
