\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S17}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Fallback emissions in the full-return training fits.} Each listed asset had one populated constant-return state containing $n_{\rm  c}$ observations. The columns $n_0$ and $n_1$ count empty and singleton states; the other $385$ full-return models required no fallback. Fallback mass is $100\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _{k\in \mathcal  F}\bar  \pi _k$, where $\mathcal  F$ contains all fallback states for that ticker. For populated constant states, the fitted location remained the state mean while the scale used the asset's full training standard deviation. Empty and singleton states used both full-series moments. $\Delta V$ is the percentage change in stationary mixture variance when only the populated constant-state scales are replaced by their empirical values, with all other parameters fixed. These are changes to the uncomposed generator, not variance errors from composition. All fits used $N=100$ and $\nu =5$.}}{38}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:fallback_assets}{{S17}{38}{}{table.17}{}}
